Prompt: how does it make a login expired though
Response: In Supabase Auth, a session expires through a two-token system: a short-lived Access Token (JWT) and a long-lived Refresh Token.

1. The Token Lifecycle
When a user logs in, Supabase issues two tokens stored in your device's persistent storage (AsyncStorage or localStorage):

Access Token (JWT): Valid for 1 hour by default. Contains the user's encrypted identity (user_id, role, email) and an expiration timestamp (exp).

Refresh Token: Valid for a longer window (default is often 7 to 30 days depending on project configuration). Its sole purpose is to request a brand new access token when the current 1-hour token runs out.

2. What Happens When the App Is Reopened
When your app launches, supabase.auth.getSession() reads the saved tokens:

Access Token Expiration Check:
Supabase decodes the JWT and inspects the payload's exp timestamp against the device's clock (Date.now() / 1000). If it has been more than 1 hour since the last request, the access token is considered expired.

Automatic Refresh Request:
Instead of immediately failing, Supabase sends a background network request to Supabase Auth servers:

HTTP
POST https://<project-ref>.supabase.co/auth/v1/token?grant_type=refresh_token
Body: { refresh_token: "..." }
When the Session Truly "Expires":
The session will fail and return session: null in any of these scenarios:

The Refresh Token Expired: The user has not opened the app within the refresh token lifetime window (e.g., 30 days).

The Refresh Token Was Revoked / Invalidation: The user logged out on another device, changed their password, or their session was deleted directly in the Supabase Dashboard.

Refresh Token Reuse Detection: Supabase uses refresh token rotation. If an old or rotated token is presented, the server revokes the entire token family for security.

User Deleted/Banned: The user record was removed or disabled in Supabase.

When any of these conditions occur, the Supabase server rejects the refresh request with a 400 / 401 Unauthorized response.

3. How to Test an Expired Session in Local Development
Because waiting 30 days for a real token to expire is impractical, you can verify your acceptance criteria using either of these approaches:

Revoke from the Supabase Dashboard:
Go to Authentication → Users in your Supabase Dashboard, find your test user, click the three dots, and select Delete User Data or Sign Out User. Reopen the app—the next refresh attempt will fail immediately.

Corrupt the Cached Storage:
In your browser dev tools (or mobile debugger), go to Application → Local Storage, find the key prefixed with sb-<project-ref>-auth-token, and either delete it or alter the refresh_token string to invalid gibberish before reloading the app.

